# interfaces are not the only option for data

data RegisterState {
    let @public high: u64;
    let @public low: u64;
    let @public widthFlag: u8;

    # No need for a 'new' function...
}

# We can define methods on RegisterState still
# We cannot, however, implement other interfaces
# Auto-dereferencing and auto-referencing apply

impl RegisterState {
    # No need to synthesise getters
}

# And then it is iniitalised like this:
RegisterState {
    .high = 0,
    .low = 123,
    .widthFlag = 2,
}

# By default, Mosaic uses these rules for primitive type size & alignment across all architectures:
#
# u8/i8/bool = size & align 1
# u16/i16 = size & align 2
# u32/i32 = size & align 4
# u64/i64 = size & align 8
# u128/i128 = size & align 16
#
# f16 = size & align 2
# f32 = size & align 4
# f64 = size & align 8
# f128 = size & align 16
#
# 8 bit vector = size & align 1
# 16 bit vector = size & align 2
# 32 bit vector = size & align 4
# 64 bit vector = size & align 8
# 128 bit vector = size & align 16
# 256 bit vector = size & align 32
#
# N[T]/[T] = sizeof & alignof T
# pointers/references = size & align 8 (no 32 bit architectures are supported)
# fat pointers & fat references = size 16, align 8
#
#
# Under the Mosaic alignment rules, the size of RegisterState is 8 * 2 + 1 = 17,
# excluding padding
# The fields are also reordered in order of alignment descending (and where two
# fields share the same alignment their relative order is the same).
# The alignment of a data is the highest alignment of its fields
# So the alignment of RegisterState is 8
# Thus, we must pad the end of RegisterState to make the size a multiple of 8
# So sizeof RegisterState = 24, and alignof RegisterState = 8
#
# Under the C alignment rules (using @calign), the size and alignment of
# RegisterState is the same. The only difference between Mosaic alignment rules
# and C alignment rules is the fact that under C alignment rules, the fields are
# not reordered.
#
# You can also disable padding using @noalign. This means the alignment of
# RegisterState would be 1, and its size would be 17. (@noalign, @align(1), and
# @calign(1) are all equivalent)
#
# You can also define a custom alignment using @align(N). This makes each field
# of the struct have that alignment, and forces the struct to have that alignment.
# For example, under @align(2), the size of RegisterState is 18, and its alignment
# 2.
#
# N must be a power of 2.
#
# Since references guarantee alignment with their inner type T, you cannot have
# a reference to a field of a data structure that is less than the type's normal
# alignment.
#
# For example, I could have a reference to &RegisterState.high if RegisterState
# had @calign, @align(8), or if it used the Mosaic alignment rules. If
# RegisterState was @noalign, @align(2), or @align(4), then you could only have
# a reference to &RegisterState.widthFlag, not to .high or .low.